design "Seatpost Number Bracket"
note "A screwless race-number holder for the back of a Giant Propel aero seatpost, held on the way rear lights are: one EPDM O-ring wraps the post with a strand at the top and one at the bottom, and each end turns round a double hook on the bracket's side (over the top prong, down behind, under the bottom one), pressing a contoured saddle and its TPU grip pad onto the post's flat back. The number's pointed end slides into the V lip of a printed tilt dial in the jaw, and a pin on a springy side arm goes through the near cheek, through the number's centre hole (where the old bolt went) and on into the stub on the far cheek that the dial turns on, so the 1 mm number cannot slip past it. To change the tilt, push the number's tail up or down: a sprung finger outside the far cheek clicks into one of three grooves under the dial at 0, 15 and 30°, with no play, and the dial is held on its keyed stub by its own shape at every click. Press the arm's lever to draw the pin back and slide the number in or out. The number's tail rises 30° above level."
note "Everything about the bracket is drawn in the seatpost's own frame and leaned back with it (rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)); the dial and the number turn about the centre hole by that angle plus rise, so the number's tail rises rise degrees above level."
note "v4, from the printed v3 and its side-arm rework: the pin spans the whole slot and goes on into the far cheek, and every overhang prints without supports (the pin and its holes are teardrops)."
note "v5: the number tilts up 30°, the bracket is 20 mm tall (the O-ring's double hooks as short as a 3.53 mm cord allows) and its seat fits a 20 mm flat back. The jaw is a plain block standing on the bed with the tilted pocket cut through it."
note "v6: the tilt adjusts by hand and is firm at each click: the number sits in a dial (a third small print) that turns about its centre hole on a stub off the far cheek and is locked by a sprung latch whose wedge teeth take up all play. The centre hole sits 3.2 mm higher so the dial's teeth fit under it in the same 20 mm height."
note "v7: the tilt is changed by pulling one big arrow-shaped tab straight out, about 7.5 N, rather than pressing a small tab sideways with about 27 N. The arrow points the way to pull, the latch springs back into the next click on its own, and a requirement keeps the pull light."
note "v8: pulling v7's latch let the dial drop off its stub, because the latch was all that held it on. Now the dial goes on a keyed stub through a keyhole that only lines up 35° past the top click, which the number cannot reach (its corner meets the seatpost first), and there is nothing to pull: a spring blade outside the far cheek presses a V finger through a window into three grooves under the dial, preloaded so there is no play. It holds twice what a 12 g pothole turns the number with, yet clicks over at about 2 N on the tail, light enough that the push does not rock the bracket on the post: a soft blade with a lot of preload does that, where a stiff one with little would need a hard push. The end grooves rise higher on their outer sides, so the end clicks are firmer and spring back. The spring is drawn unbent, pressing into the dial by its preload (flexure preload: against:), which the overlap checks take as its squeeze."

brief {
  purpose: "Hold a race number behind a Giant Propel aero seatpost with no screws"
  setting: "Road and triathlon racing: fitted and taken off by hand at the bike, ridden in rain and sun"
  priorities: ["number never comes out: the pin goes through it into the far side", "the dial cannot come off, at any click or while it is turned", "tilt clicks by pushing the number's tail, with no play", "anyone can change the tilt without being shown how", "prints with no supports at all", "compact", "fits by hand with one O-ring", "does not rock or turn on the post"]
  look: "Black, small, tucked in behind the post"

  temp: -5C .. 50C
  outdoor: true
  exposure: ["sunlight", "rain", "vibration", "impact"]

  process: "FDM print"
  material: "ASA"
  quantity: 1

  use gust "Holds the number in a 70 km/h crosswind gust" { on: bracket }
  use knock "Survives a leg or hand knocking the number's tail" { on: bracket }
  use bumps "Stays put over potholes (about 12 g)" { on: bracket }
  use fit "Goes on and comes off by hand, no tools" { on: bracket }
  use adjust "Tilts by pushing the number's tail, in 15° clicks, and stays put with no play" { on: bracket }

  given post_w = 20mm "Seatpost width, side to side at its widest (taken as no wider than its flat back)" assumed
  given post_d = 36mm "Seatpost depth, front to back" assumed
  given tail_w = 20mm "Width of the post's flat back: at least 20 mm, measured on the bike"
  given tilt = 16.5 "How far the seatpost leans back from vertical, degrees (73.5° seat angle)" assumed
  given plate_l = 122mm "Number length, read from the photo" assumed
  given plate_h = 69mm "Number height, read from the photo" assumed
  given plate_t = 1mm "Number thickness" assumed
  given taper_l = 10mm "Length of the number's pointed end, read from the photo" assumed
  given tip_h = 6mm "Flat at the very point (under the old bracket's arm in the photo)" assumed
  given hole_c = 4.5mm "From the point to the centre hole (under the old bracket's washer in the photo)" assumed
  given hole_cd = 5.2mm "Centre hole, which takes the old bracket's M5 bolt" assumed
  given hole_d = 3.5mm "The other holes, read from the photo" assumed
  given hole_in = 11.5mm "From the point to the corner holes, read from the photo" assumed
  given hole_z = 19mm "Corner holes above and below the middle, read from the photo" assumed

  reference photo "The number on its old bolted bracket" { notes: "Race Result plate 4004, tag-shaped with a blunt pointed end; the old bracket's M5 bolt goes through the centre hole at the point, two more holes 38 mm apart about 7 mm behind it, two along the top edge; yellow stripe at the tail, timing transponder sticker across the top half, 4004 in heavy black digits with a small leading 4" }
  reference propel "Giant Propel seatpost" { url: "https://www.giant-bicycles.com/", notes: "Giant publishes no section drawing of the aero seatpost: measure its width, depth and flat back and set the givens" }
  reference lights "How rear lights hold on with one band" { notes: "dan's photo of a rear light: one rubber loop wraps the post with a strand high and a strand low, and each end hooks onto a double hook on the light's side, the upper hook turned up and the lower one down, so the band cannot rock the light about it. Garmin Varia's mount adds a rubber shim shaped to the post (round or aero), keyed into the bracket; riders on D-shaped posts report it turning unless the shim fits the post" }

  assume "The number is polypropylene, about 30 MPa yield"
  assume "Painted carbon post: EPDM grips it with μ 0.8 dry, 0.5 wet; the TPU pad about 0.5 wet; bare ASA only about 0.3"
}

// ── The saddle on the post ─────────────────────────────
param saddle_h = 20mm   range 20 .. 50  "Height along the post of the saddle, its double hooks and the jaw: the whole bracket"
param saddle_t = 6mm    range 4 .. 10   "Saddle thickness behind the post"
param horn     = 4mm    range 0 .. 8    "How far the saddle wraps round the post's back corners"
param wall     = 2.2mm  range 1.6 .. 4  "Wall beside the post"
param pad_t    = 1.5mm  range 1.2 .. 2.5 "Grip pad thickness (TPU)"

// ── The O-ring and its double hooks ────────────────────
param oring_id = 53.57mm range 40 .. 70 "O-ring inside diameter (AS568-227 is 53.57 mm)"
param oring_cs = 3.53mm  range 2.62 .. 3.53 "O-ring cord (AS568-2xx is 3.53 mm)"
param hook_x   = 18.5mm range 13 .. 24  "Double hooks out from the middle"
param hook_y   = 4mm    range 2 .. 8    "Prongs behind the post's flat back"
param prong_d  = 5.5mm  range 3 .. 8    "Prong diameter: the bend the cord makes (at least 1.5 cords, so the O-ring does not wear through)"
param lip      = 2.7mm  range 1 .. 4    "Rim round each double hook (the stretch to get the O-ring's end over it)"

// ── The jaw, its locking pin and the tilt dial ─────────
param pivot_y  = 24mm   range 20 .. 32  "From the post's flat back to the number's centre hole, where the pin goes and the number turns"
param rise     = 30     range 0 .. 30   "How far the number's tail rises above level, degrees: the dial clicks at 0, 15 and 30"
param cheek    = 2.5mm  range 2 .. 4    "Jaw cheek thickness"
param arm_t    = 1.2mm  range 1.2 .. 2  "Locking arm thickness"
param pin_d    = 3.2mm  range 2.4 .. 3.6 "Locking pin diameter (it and its teardrop point go through the 5.2 mm centre hole)"
param engage   = 1mm    range 0.6 .. 2  "How far the pin goes on into the stub on the far cheek"
param dial_t   = 3mm    range 2.6 .. 4  "Tilt dial thickness: it turns on the stub over all of it, and its click grooves are cut in its far face"
param flank    = 55     range 45 .. 60  "Slope of the click grooves' sides and the finger's V from the dial's face, degrees: steeper holds harder for the same spring; much past 60° friction locks it"
param squeeze  = 0.8mm  range 0.4 .. 1   "How far the click spring stays bent with its finger in a groove: its preload, which takes up all play. More preload with a softer blade holds as hard but clicks over more easily"
param spring_t = 2mm    range 1.8 .. 3  "Click spring blade thickness"

// ── Duty ───────────────────────────────────────────────
param gust     = 70kmh  range 30kmh .. 120kmh "Crosswind gust"
param knock    = 25N    range 5 .. 60   "A leg or hand knocking the number's tail"
param bump_g   = 12     range 3 .. 25   "Pothole shock, in g"

let slot     = plate_t + 0.4                         // in front of the dial the number runs in a slot 0.2 mm each side of it: a narrow slot prints a little undersize
let jaw_w    = slot + 2 * cheek                      // the near cheek stands from slot / 2 to jaw_w / 2
let seat_off = pad_t + 0.2                           // the saddle's seat stands off the post by the pad and a little room
let sw       = tail_w + 2 * (seat_off + wall)        // saddle width
let pad_mu   = 0.5                                   // TPU on wet painted carbon
// the number's outline: its point at (0, 0), running back along +u, up +v; and the same about its centre hole
let tag      = polygon(points: [(0, -tip_h / 2), (taper_l, -plate_h / 2), (plate_l, -plate_h / 2), (plate_l, plate_h / 2), (taper_l, plate_h / 2), (0, tip_h / 2)], r: 3)
let tag_h    = polygon(points: [(-hole_c, -tip_h / 2), (taper_l - hole_c, -plate_h / 2), (plate_l - hole_c, -plate_h / 2), (plate_l - hole_c, plate_h / 2), (taper_l - hole_c, plate_h / 2), (-hole_c, tip_h / 2)], r: 3)
// the post's section: a round nose and a straight taper to its flat back at y = 0
let nose_y   = -post_d + post_w / 2
let post_sec = circle(post_w, at: (0, nose_y)) + polygon(points: [(-post_w / 2, nose_y), (post_w / 2, nose_y), (tail_w / 2, 0), (-tail_w / 2, 0)], r: 1.5)

// the tilt dial: a plate beside the number that turns on a keyed stub off the far cheek, with a lip in the number's own
// plane round its point (a V pocket, so the number turns with it). The click spring holds the lip hard against the near
// cheek, so the number's gap in the jaw is the lip's height, 0.1 mm each side of it: the dial prints on its far face, so
// that height comes out true to the layer (4.2 mm in all, 21 layers of 0.2). Under its hub, a sector as big as the
// bracket's height allows carries the click grooves, so the number's centre hole sits a little above the bracket's middle
let lip_t    = plate_t + 0.2
let x_near   = slot / 2                              // the near cheek's inner face
let x_dn     = x_near - lip_t                        // the dial's near face, beside the number
let x_bed    = x_dn - dial_t                         // its far face, which it prints on
let far_in   = x_bed - 0.3                           // the far cheek's inner face: a knock on the number pushes the dial back no further than this
let far_out  = far_in - cheek
let H_z      = 2.8
let r_hub    = saddle_h / 2 - H_z - 0.2              // the dial's hub, up to the bracket's top
let r_sec    = 11                                    // its click sector, under the hub
let r_lobe   = 8.6                                   // its lobe in front, under the lip
let r_lip    = r_lobe - 0.2
let lip_a    = 180 - acos((hole_c - 0.5) / r_lip) + 6

// the number turns about its centre hole H: by the post's lean and on by rise, so its tail rises rise degrees above
// level; its point J sits hole_c in front of H
let H        = (0, pivot_y, H_z)
let ja       = tilt + rise
let ca       = cos(ja)
let sa       = sin(ja)
let jaw_y    = pivot_y - hole_c * ca
let J        = (0, jaw_y, H_z - hole_c * sa)
let ct       = cos(tilt)
let st       = sin(tilt)
// where J, H and the post's axis end up in the world once everything leans back
let J_w      = (0, jaw_y * ct + J.z * st, -jaw_y * st + J.z * ct)
let H_w      = (0, pivot_y * ct + H_z * st, -pivot_y * st + H_z * ct)
let post_ax  = (0, st, ct)
let post_back = (0, ct, -st)
let post_mid = (0, (nose_y / 2) * ct - (saddle_h / 2 - 1.5) * st, -(nose_y / 2) * st - (saddle_h / 2 - 1.5) * ct)
// the way the number runs back, in the world
let num_dir  = (0, cos(rise), sin(rise))
// the dial clicks every 15°; at 30° the number's top front corner is as close to the post as it may come
let clicks   = [0, 15, 30]
let top_click = 30
let ja_t     = tilt + top_click
// tilted up, the number's top front corner swings forward toward the post: how far behind the post's flat back it stays
let corner_y = pivot_y + (taper_l - hole_c) * cos(ja_t) - plate_h / 2 * sin(ja_t)
// where the number's top front edge leaves the top of the jaw at the top click
let jaw_t    = pivot_y - hole_c * cos(ja_t)
let jz_t     = H_z - hole_c * sin(ja_t)
let edge_y   = taper_l * cos(ja_t) - (plate_h - tip_h) / 2 * sin(ja_t)
let edge_z   = taper_l * sin(ja_t) + (plate_h - tip_h) / 2 * cos(ja_t)
let top_open = jaw_t - tip_h / 2 * sin(ja_t) + (saddle_h / 2 - jz_t - tip_h / 2 * cos(ja_t)) * edge_y / edge_z

// the stub the dial turns on: flats 7.2 mm apart, its top rounded and its underside a 50° point, so it prints with no
// support and keeps 1.2 mm round the pin's hole. The dial's bore opens in a keyhole that passes the flats only when the dial
// is turned fit_turn past its top click, where the number cannot go (its top front corner would be inside the seatpost),
// so the dial is held on by its own shape at every click and while it turns
let st_w     = 3.6
let st_r     = 5.4
let st_c     = sqrt(st_r^2 - st_w^2)
let st_v     = st_r - st_w * tan(50)
let stub_sec = outline([(pivot_y + st_w, H_z - st_v), (pivot_y + st_w, H_z + st_c), arc_to((pivot_y - st_w, H_z + st_c), via: (pivot_y, H_z + st_r)), (pivot_y - st_w, H_z - st_v), (pivot_y, H_z - st_r)])
let x_stub   = x_dn - 0.1                            // the stub's end, just inside the dial's near face
let bore_r   = st_r + 0.15
let key_w    = 2 * st_w + 0.3
let key_h    = asin(key_w / 2 / bore_r)             // the keyhole's sides are radial, so the hub's ends are square, not knife edges
let fit_turn = 35
let ja_fit   = tilt + top_click + fit_turn
let key_a    = 90 - ja_fit                           // the keyhole's way in the dial's own frame: straight up at the fitting turn, so the dial slides up onto the stub from below
let key_slip = asin(key_w / 2 / st_r) - acos(st_c / st_r)    // how far either side of the fitting turn it still slides off
// the clicks: a spring blade outside the far cheek pushes a V finger through a window in it, straight down from the centre
// hole, into one of three V grooves 15° apart in the dial's far face. Between the end grooves the face is cut back by tr_d,
// so the finger rides out only ride to pass to the next click; the end grooves' outer sides run on up to the face, so the
// end clicks are firmer and spring back. Drawn unbent, the finger sits squeeze deeper than its groove: the spring's preload
let tr_d     = 0.4
let ride     = 0.5
let pt_gap   = 0.5                                   // the finger's point stops this far short of the grooves' bottom, so it is a flat 0.7 mm wide
let x_apex   = x_bed + tr_d + ride + pt_gap          // the grooves' bottom
let gw       = (x_apex - x_bed + 0.1) / tan(flank)   // half a groove's width at the far face
let r_tr     = bore_r + 1.3                          // the track and grooves start here, outside the hub's ring round the bore
let r_in     = r_tr + 0.4                            // the finger's top
let r_c      = (r_in + r_sec) / 2                    // where the finger bears, on average
let groove_a = -90 - tilt                            // the 0° click's groove in the dial's own frame; the others turn on from it
let sec_hi   = groove_a + 15                         // the sector runs from past the 0° groove's outer side
let sec_lo   = -90 - ja_fit - 8                      // to past where the finger rests at the fitting turn
let cav_front = pivot_y + r_sec * cos(sec_lo + tilt) - 0.5
let cheek_back = pivot_y + 7                         // the cheeks run on past the dial
// the click spring, rooted in the saddle like the arm, with its finger level with the centre hole; stubby, so the click's
// sideways push hardly bends it. Its tab at the tip is only for fitting the dial
let spring_root = seat_off + 1.3
let spring_a = pivot_y - spring_root
let blade_in = far_out - 0.8                         // the blade's inner face, clear of the far cheek
let blade_x  = blade_in - spring_t / 2
let f_w      = 5
let f_tip    = x_apex + squeeze - pt_gap
let f_base   = x_apex + squeeze - f_w / 2 * tan(flank)
let f_flat   = pt_gap / tan(flank)
let finger   = [(blade_in - 0.1, pivot_y - f_w / 2), (f_base, pivot_y - f_w / 2), (f_tip, pivot_y - f_flat), (f_tip, pivot_y + f_flat), (f_base, pivot_y + f_w / 2), (blade_in - 0.1, pivot_y + f_w / 2)]
let fit_travel = f_tip - x_bed + 0.3                 // drawn back this far, the finger clears the dial's face to fit it
let tab_y    = cheek_back - 1.25
// the spring's rate at the finger (ASA's modulus 1900 MPa); the finger pushes below the blade's middle, so the blade also
// twists (shear modulus about 700 MPa)
let sp_bend  = 3 * 1900 * (saddle_h * spring_t^3 / 12) / spring_a^3
let sp_twist = (H_z - r_c)^2 * spring_a / (700 * saddle_h * spring_t^3 / 3)
let k_click  = 1 / (1 / sp_bend + sp_twist)
let tf       = tan(flank + 16.7)                     // ASA slides on ASA at about μ 0.3, 16.7°
let tail_arm = plate_l - hole_c                      // the number's tail from its centre hole
let hold_t   = k_click * squeeze * r_c * tf          // N·mm the dial holds before it starts to turn
let click_n  = k_click * (squeeze + ride) * r_c * tf / tail_arm
let end_n    = k_click * (squeeze + ride + tr_d) * r_c * tf / tail_arm
let pothole_t = 0.011 * 9.81 * bump_g * (plate_l / 2 - hole_c)    // the 11 g number at bump_g, about its centre hole
// pulled at its tab, the spring draws the finger back by fit_travel
let fit_n    = sp_bend * 2 * spring_a / (3 * (tab_y - spring_root) - spring_a) * fit_travel
// the number's outline as it turns from click to click (every 5°), cut through the jaw in front of the dial
let tag_o    = offset(tag_h, 0.3)
let sweep    = rotate(tag_o, tilt) + rotate(tag_o, tilt + 5) + rotate(tag_o, tilt + 10) + rotate(tag_o, tilt + 15) + rotate(tag_o, tilt + 20) + rotate(tag_o, tilt + 25) + rotate(tag_o, tilt + 30)
// the locking arm: a blade beside the near (+x) cheek, rooted in the saddle and running back past the hole; its pin goes
// through the near cheek, the number and on into the stub. Drawing it back by pull frees the slot
let arm_gap  = 0.6                                   // between the arm and the cheek: open enough not to fuse when printed
let arm_x    = jaw_w / 2 + arm_gap + arm_t / 2
let arm_root = seat_off + 1.3                        // in the saddle, 1.3 mm behind its seat
let pin_at   = H.y - arm_root
let arm_l    = pin_at + pin_d / 2 + 0.5
let reach    = arm_gap + cheek + x_near - x_stub + engage
let pull     = x_near - x_stub + engage + 0.3
// the tip moves further than the pin does: a cantilever's deflection at a is δ a²(3L − a) / 2L³
let arm_travel = pull * 2 * arm_l^3 / (pin_at^2 * (3 * arm_l - pin_at))
// the pin's holes: round with 0.35 mm all round (any less fuses shut when printed), pointed up so their tops print
// without support and down to clear the pin's own teardrop point. As the arm is drawn back it tips the pin back a
// little, so the holes run on that way: most in the near cheek, which the pin stays in
let hd       = pin_d + 0.7
let hk       = hd / 2 * 0.7071
let ha       = hd / 2 * 1.4142
let near_e   = 1.2
let far_e    = 0.4
let near_hole = circle(hd) + circle(hd, at: (near_e, 0)) + polygon(points: [(-hk, -hk), (-hk, hk), (near_e / 2, ha + near_e / 2), (near_e + hk, hk), (near_e + hk, -hk), (near_e / 2, -ha - near_e / 2)])
let far_hole = circle(hd) + circle(hd, at: (far_e, 0)) + polygon(points: [(-hk, -hk), (-hk, hk), (far_e / 2, ha + far_e / 2), (far_e + hk, hk), (far_e + hk, -hk), (far_e / 2, -ha - far_e / 2)])
// a lever off the arm's tip, back and out at 45°: a thumb pressing it toward the post draws the pin back
let lever_x  = jaw_w / 2 + arm_gap
let lever_y  = arm_root + arm_l
let lever_l  = 3.2                                   // 4.5 mm long, 1.6 mm thick
// the saddle seat: a cylinder zone round the post's axis that takes in the seat but not the saddle's back or the wings
let seat_r   = hypot(tail_w / 2, nose_y / 2) + seat_off
// each double hook: a block in a groove between a wing off the saddle and an outer rim; the block's rounded back corners are its two prongs
let groove   = oring_cs + 1.5
let rim_t    = 1.6
let spread   = saddle_h - prong_d - 2 * lip            // the prongs' centres apart: the rims come flush with the saddle's top and bottom
let block_d  = hook_y + prong_d / 2                    // the block's back face behind the post's flat back
let hook_in  = sw / 2 - 1
let hook_out = hook_x + groove / 2
// seen from the side (y back, z up): the block, whose rounded back corners are the prongs, and the rim and stop round it,
// lip proud. The lower prong's underside is chamfered at 45° below its middle, so it prints without support
let pr       = prong_d / 2
let block    = outline([(0, -spread / 2 - pr), (hook_y + 0.414 * pr, -spread / 2 - pr), (hook_y + 0.707 * pr, -spread / 2 - 0.707 * pr), arc_to((hook_y + pr, -spread / 2), via: (hook_y + 0.924 * pr, -spread / 2 - 0.383 * pr)), (hook_y + pr, spread / 2), arc_to((hook_y, spread / 2 + pr), via: (hook_y + 0.707 * pr, spread / 2 + 0.707 * pr)), (0, spread / 2 + pr)])
// (the rims' tops already come flush with the saddle's, so the rect only trims their front). Under the lower prong's
// back corner the offset rounds off into the bed; a step down to the bed there keeps that rounding from hanging out
let corner_x = hook_y + 0.414 * pr + 0.7071 * lip
let corner_z = -spread / 2 - pr - 0.7071 * lip
let hook_face = overlap(offset(block, lip) + rect(corner_x, corner_z + saddle_h / 2 + 0.5, at: (corner_x / 2, (corner_z - saddle_h / 2 - 0.5) / 2)), rect(block_d + lip, saddle_h + 2, at: ((block_d + lip) / 2, 0)))
// how far the saddle's front corner stands inside the strand that runs from a hook to the widest of the post's nose
let run_x    = post_w / 2 - hook_x
let run_y    = nose_y - hook_y
let clear    = (run_y * (sw / 2 - hook_x) - run_x * (-horn - hook_y)) / hypot(run_x, run_y)

part post {
  label: "Seatpost (Giant Propel)"
  material: "Carbon fiber"
  finish: "gloss"
  color: #17181b
  group: "Bike"
  process: false
  rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)
  body extrude(post_sec, 150, at: (0, 0, -70))
}

part bracket {
  label: "Number bracket"
  color: #222428
  material: "ASA"
  group: "Bracket"
  rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)
  // printed standing up as drawn: the saddle's foot, both wings and the neck stand on the bed, nothing hangs out
  // saddle: a block behind the post with its front cut to the post's own shape, standing off it by the pad
  body box(sw, saddle_t + horn, saddle_h, at: (0, (saddle_t - horn) / 2, -saddle_h / 2)) as saddle
  fillet(1.5, edges: "vertical")
  // a double hook on each side, the way a rear light's band holds: the O-ring's end goes over the top prong, down behind
  // the block and under the bottom prong, between a stop and an outer rim that stand lip proud of it. The pull draws the
  // end inward onto the stop; the rim keeps it from sliding off. Block, stop and rim stand on the bed and the block's
  // underside bridges between them, so it prints without support
  for side in [-1, 1] {
    add extrude(block, hook_out - hook_in, at: (side * (hook_in + hook_out) / 2 - (hook_out - hook_in) / 2, 0, 0), dir: X) as prongs
    add extrude(hook_face, rim_t, at: (side * (hook_out + rim_t / 2) - rim_t / 2, 0, 0), dir: X) as rims
    // the stop runs from the saddle's side to the groove and starts behind the prongs, so the strand running forward and in
    // toward the post clears it
    add extrude(overlap(hook_face, rect(block_d + lip - hook_y, saddle_h + 2, at: ((block_d + lip + hook_y) / 2, 0))), hook_x - groove / 2 - hook_in, at: (side * (hook_in + hook_x - groove / 2) / 2 - (hook_x - groove / 2 - hook_in) / 2, 0, 0), dir: X) as stops
  }
  // the jaw: a block standing on the bed from the saddle back past the pin, the full height of the saddle; on the arm's side
  // it stops at the near cheek
  add box(jaw_w / 2 - far_out, cheek_back - saddle_t + 1, saddle_h, at: ((jaw_w / 2 + far_out) / 2, (saddle_t - 1 + cheek_back) / 2, -saddle_h / 2)) as jaw
  // the seat cut last of the solid, so nothing added reaches into it
  cut extrude(offset(post_sec, seat_off), 2 * saddle_h + 2, at: (0, 0, -saddle_h - 1)) as seat
  // a stopped groove for the pad's key, so the pad cannot slide or turn in the saddle
  cut box(3.4, 1.3, saddle_h - 7.4, at: (0, seat_off + 0.55, -(saddle_h - 7.4) / 2)) as key_slot
  // the cavity the dial and the number's point turn in, between the cheeks, from just in front of the dial out through the
  // top, the bottom and the back
  cut box(x_near - far_in, cheek_back + 2 - cav_front, saddle_h + 2, at: ((x_near + far_in) / 2, (cav_front + cheek_back + 2) / 2, -saddle_h / 2 - 1)) as cavity
  // in front of it, the slot the number's outline runs on into as it turns from click to click
  cut extrude(sweep, slot, at: (-slot / 2, pivot_y, H_z), dir: X) as slot_cut
  // the window in the far cheek the click spring's finger reaches through, from the bed up: its top is a short bridge
  cut box(cheek + 0.2, f_w + 0.6, saddle_h / 2 + H_z - r_in + 1.3, at: ((far_in + far_out) / 2, pivot_y, -saddle_h / 2 - 1)) as window
  // the stub the dial turns on, off the far cheek's inner face to just inside the dial's near face; its flats stand
  // upright and its underside is a 50° point, so it prints without support
  add extrude(stub_sec, x_stub - far_in + 0.5, at: (far_in - 0.5, 0, 0), dir: X) as stub
  // the pin's holes: through the near cheek at the number's centre hole, and on into the stub
  cut extrude(near_hole, jaw_w / 2 + arm_gap / 2, at: (0, H.y, H.z), dir: X) as near_hole_cut
  cut extrude(far_hole, engage + 0.6, at: (x_stub - engage - 0.5, H.y, H.z), dir: X) as far_hole_cut
  // slits either side of the arm's and the click spring's roots, so they bend from inside the saddle and are long enough not to strain
  cut box(arm_t + 2 * arm_gap, saddle_t + 1 - arm_root, saddle_h + 2, at: (arm_x, (arm_root + saddle_t + 1) / 2, -saddle_h / 2 - 1)) as arm_slit
  cut box(spring_t + 1.2, saddle_t + 1 - spring_root, saddle_h + 2, at: (blade_x, (spring_root + saddle_t + 1) / 2, -saddle_h / 2 - 1)) as spring_slit
  // the locking arm stands on the bed beside the near cheek, its pin level with the number's centre hole; the pin has a
  // teardrop point underneath, so it prints level
  add flexure((arm_x, arm_root, 0), arm_l, arm_t, saddle_h, dir: Y, facing: -X, travel: arm_travel, cycles: 500, root_r: 0.3, pin: pin_d, reach: reach, pin_at: (arm_x, H.y, H.z), teardrop: -Z) as arm
  add prism(points: [(lever_x, lever_y), (lever_x + lever_l, lever_y + lever_l), (lever_x + lever_l + 1.13, lever_y + lever_l - 1.13), (lever_x + 1.13, lever_y - 1.13)], h: saddle_h, at: (0, 0, -saddle_h / 2)) as lever
  // the click spring stands on the bed beside the far cheek. Drawn unbent, its finger presses squeeze into the dial's
  // groove: that is its preload, and the dial is drawn where the spring holds it, its lip against the near cheek
  add flexure((blade_x, spring_root, 0), spring_a, spring_t, saddle_h, dir: Y, facing: X, travel: fit_travel, cycles: 10, root_r: 0.3, preload: squeeze, against: "dial") as click_spring
  // on past the finger to the back of the cheeks, with a tab at its tip to pull it out by when fitting the dial
  add box(spring_t, cheek_back - pivot_y + 0.1, saddle_h, at: (blade_x, (pivot_y - 0.1 + cheek_back) / 2, -saddle_h / 2)) as spring_tip
  add box(3.1, 2.5, saddle_h, at: (blade_in - spring_t - 1.45, tab_y, -saddle_h / 2)) as fit_tab
  // the finger: standing on the bed and through the window, its V point in the dial's groove
  add prism(points: finger, h: saddle_h / 2 + H_z - r_in, at: (0, 0, -saddle_h / 2)) as finger_v
  // flat underneath, level with the saddle's foot, so it stands on the bed
  cut box(120, 120, 40, at: (0, 0, -saddle_h / 2 - 40)) as foot
}

// the rubber shim a rear light's bracket has: a TPU liner shaped to the post's back and corners
part pad {
  label: "Grip pad"
  material: "TPU"
  color: #3b3e44
  group: "Bracket"
  rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)
  // printed standing up as drawn, like the bracket
  body extrude(overlap(offset(post_sec, 0.05 + pad_t) - offset(post_sec, 0.05), rect(sw + 2, horn + pad_t + 1, at: (0, (pad_t + 1 - horn) / 2))), saddle_h, at: (0, 0, -saddle_h / 2)) as liner
  // a key down its back drops into the saddle's groove
  add box(3, 1, saddle_h - 8, at: (0, pad_t + 0.5, -(saddle_h - 8) / 2)) as key
}

// ── The tilt dial ───────────────────────────────────────
// its outlines are drawn about the number's centre hole, with +x along the number; the part is drawn in the bracket's frame,
// turned about the centre hole by the post's lean and on by rise, so it turns with the number
let lobe     = overlap(circle(2 * r_lobe), rect(r_lobe + 1 - hole_c + 0.7, 2 * r_lobe + 2, at: ((-r_lobe - 1 - hole_c + 0.7) / 2, 0)))
let sector   = overlap(circle(2 * r_sec), polygon(points: [(0, 0), (15 * cos(sec_lo), 15 * sin(sec_lo)), (15 * cos((sec_lo + sec_hi) / 2), 15 * sin((sec_lo + sec_hi) / 2)), (15 * cos(sec_hi), 15 * sin(sec_hi))]))
// the track between the end grooves, cut tr_d into the far face
let track    = overlap(circle(2 * r_sec + 2) - circle(2 * r_tr), polygon(points: [(0, 0), (15 * cos(groove_a - 30), 15 * sin(groove_a - 30)), (15 * cos(groove_a - 15), 15 * sin(groove_a - 15)), (15 * cos(groove_a), 15 * sin(groove_a))]))

part dial {
  label: "Tilt dial"
  color: #222428
  material: "ASA"
  group: "Bracket"
  process: mfg.fdm(build: X)
  rotate: (-tilt, 0, 0)
  // printed on its far face: the bore and keyhole go straight through, the click grooves' sides slope at flank and the
  // track between them is a short bridge, so nothing hangs out (shrunk and grown back by 0.6 mm, which leaves nothing
  // thinner than 1.2 mm)
  body rotate(extrude(offset(offset(circle(2 * r_hub) + lobe + sector, -0.6), 0.6) - circle(2 * bore_r) - rotate(polygon(points: [(0, 0), (12 * cos(key_h), -12 * sin(key_h)), (12, 0), (12 * cos(key_h), 12 * sin(key_h))]), key_a), dial_t, at: (x_bed, pivot_y, H_z), dir: X), ja, axis: X, origin: H) as disc
  // the lip in front of the number's point, in the number's own plane: a V pocket 0.2 mm round its outline
  add rotate(extrude(overlap(circle(2 * r_lip) - offset(tag_h, 0.2), polygon(points: [(0, 0), (12 * cos(lip_a), 12 * sin(lip_a)), (-12, 12), (-12, -12), (12 * cos(lip_a), -12 * sin(lip_a))])), lip_t + 0.1, at: (x_dn - 0.1, pivot_y, H_z), dir: X), ja, axis: X, origin: H) as v_lip
  // the click grooves in its far face, one for each click, where the spring's finger is at that click
  cut rotate(extrude(track, tr_d + 0.1, at: (x_bed - 0.1, pivot_y, H_z), dir: X), ja, axis: X, origin: H) as track_cut
  for c in clicks {
    cut rotate(prism(points: [(-x_apex, 0), (-x_bed + 0.1, gw), (-x_bed + 0.1, -gw)], h: r_sec + 1 - r_tr, at: (0, pivot_y + r_tr, H_z), dir: Y), ja + groove_a - c, axis: X, origin: H) as grooves
  }
}

// ── The race number, as the Race Result plate it is ─────
// each of its parts is drawn with the point at the origin, then tilted up by rise and moved to the jaw
let ink      = offset(text("004", size: 31, at: (4.4, 0)), 0.45) + offset(text("4", size: 14, at: (-29, -6.2)), 0.25)
let ink_at   = (0, 59.5, -17.5)
let sticker  = rect(105, 28, r: 2, at: (67, 12.5))
let stripe   = overlap(tag, rect(12, plate_h + 2, at: (plate_l - 6, 0)))

part number {
  label: "Race number (4004)"
  material: "PP"
  color: #f4f4f0
  group: "Number"
  process: false
  rotate: (rise, 0, 0)
  held: "pinned through its centre hole by the sprung arm's pin, which runs on into the stub on the far cheek"
  at: J_w
  body extrude(tag, plate_t, at: (-plate_t / 2, 0, 0), dir: X) as plate
  // the centre hole at the point, where the old bracket's bolt went and the locking pin now goes
  cut hole(hole_cd, through: true, at: (plate_t / 2, hole_c, 0), dir: -X)
  for zz in [-hole_z, hole_z] {
    cut hole(hole_d, through: true, at: (plate_t / 2, hole_in, zz), dir: -X)
  }
  // two more along its top edge, for brackets that tie it on
  for uu in [49, 70] {
    cut hole(hole_d, through: true, at: (plate_t / 2, uu, plate_h / 2 - 4), dir: -X)
  }
}

// printed on both faces; each reads from its own side
part number_print {
  label: "Race number print"
  material: "PP"
  color: #121214
  group: "Number"
  process: false
  rotate: (rise, 0, 0)
  at: J_w
  body emboss(ink, at: ink_at + (-plate_t / 2 - 0.05, 0, 0), facing: -X, h: 0.03)
  add emboss(ink, at: ink_at + (plate_t / 2 + 0.05, 0, 0), facing: X, h: 0.03)
}

part number_stripe {
  label: "Race number stripe"
  material: "PP"
  color: #f2c200
  group: "Number"
  process: false
  rotate: (rise, 0, 0)
  at: J_w
  body extrude(stripe, 0.05, at: (plate_t / 2, 0, 0), dir: X)
  add extrude(stripe - sticker, 0.05, at: (-plate_t / 2 - 0.05, 0, 0), dir: X)
}

// a foil antenna under clear film: silver-grey, not shiny metal
part transponder {
  label: "Timing transponder sticker"
  material: "PP"
  finish: "gloss"
  color: #c9cac6
  group: "Number"
  process: false
  rotate: (rise, 0, 0)
  at: J_w
  body extrude(sticker, 0.1, at: (-plate_t / 2 - 0.1, 0, 0), dir: X)
}

// ── The O-ring: its end on the left double hook, round the post high and low, its other end over the right hook's rim ──
component oring = lib.band("EPDM", id: oring_id, cs: oring_cs, around: post, holds: bracket, from: (-hook_x, hook_y, 0), to: (hook_x, hook_y, 0), pin: prong_d, lip: lip, spread: spread, open: [(-1, 0, 0), (1, 0, 0)], carries: 30g, shock: bump_g, saddle_mu: pad_mu) {
  group: "Bands"
}

decision holding "How it holds the post" {
  ask: "Hold the bracket on without a screw"
  chose: "One O-ring on double hooks"
  by: "you"
  why: "How the rear light in your photo does it: one band wraps the post with a strand high and a strand low, and each end turns round a double hook on the bracket's side, so the band also stops the bracket rocking about it. A rubber pad shaped to the post lets one band's squeeze hold it. A closed band cannot get round a seatpost that carries on above and below, so the O-ring's end goes on one hook, wraps the post and is stretched over the other hook's rim."
  option "One O-ring on double hooks" "One O-ring to fit; its two strands, 18 mm apart, hold the bracket square on the post."
  option "One O-ring on two pins" "Simpler hooks, but both strands sit at mid-height, so the bracket can rock about them."
  option "Clamp with a screw" "Holds hardest, but needs a tool and can mark the post."
}

decision seat_grip "What grips the post" {
  ask: "Stop the bracket turning on the post with only one O-ring"
  chose: "Printed TPU pad"
  by: "claude"
  why: "Bare ASA on painted carbon grips at about μ 0.3; a rubbery pad about 0.5 even wet, so one O-ring holds what two did. Keyed into a groove so it cannot slip out, like the shim in a rear light's bracket."
  option "Printed TPU pad" "A second small print in TPU; replaceable."
  option "Strip of old inner tube" "Cut to fit and stick in with double-sided tape: no TPU needed, grips as well."
  option "Bare plastic" "Nothing extra, but the knock check fails with one O-ring."
}

decision number_hold "How the number is held" {
  ask: "Keep the number in without a screw"
  chose: "Pin through both cheeks"
  by: "you"
  why: "It mounts where the old bracket's bolt did, at the centre hole in the point. The point nests in a pocket of its own shape, so it cannot turn about the pin. A test print showed that a peg that only reaches into the hole lets a 1 mm number slip past its tip, so the pin on the springy side arm goes right through the number and on into the stub on the far cheek: it is held on both sides and the number can only come out by shearing it. The point nests in the tilt dial's lip, so it turns with the dial and not about the pin."
  option "Pin through both cheeks" "Press the lever, slide the number in and let go; the pin goes through it into the far cheek's stub."
  option "Click peg in the centre hole" "Slides in until it clicks, but the peg only reaches into the hole, so a thin number can slip past it."
  option "Bolt through the centre hole" "What it had: needs a nut and a spanner."
  option "Zip ties through the corner holes" "No print, but cut off and replaced every race."
}

decision number_angle "How the number sits" {
  ask: "Angle the number"
  chose: "Tail up 30°"
  by: "you"
  why: "You asked for the number to tilt up about 30°, then to be able to change it. The dial clicks at 0, 15 and 30°; this is the click the design is shown and checked at, and every click is checked to fit. Tilted up, the number's top front corner swings toward the post, so its centre hole sits 24 mm behind the post's flat back to keep that corner clear at the top click."
  option "Tail up 30°" { rise: 30 } "What you asked for: the dial's top click."
  option "Tail up 15°" { rise: 15 } "The middle click."
  option "Level" { rise: 0 } "The bottom click: straight back, as the old bracket held it."
}

decision tilt_lock "How the tilt is set and held" {
  ask: "Adjust the tilt without it being loose, and without the dial coming off"
  chose: "Push-to-click dial on a keyed stub"
  by: "claude"
  why: "Your v7 dial dropped off its stub when its latch was pulled to turn it, because the latch was all that held it on. Now there is nothing to pull: push the number's tail and it clicks to the next tilt. A spring blade outside the far cheek presses a V finger into one of three grooves under the dial, preloaded so there is no play; it holds twice what a 12 g pothole turns the number with, yet clicks over at about 2 N on the tail, so the push does not rock the bracket on the post, and the end clicks are firmer and spring back if pushed a little past. The dial goes on its stub through a keyhole that only lines up 35° past the top click, where the number cannot go (it would be inside the seatpost), so the dial is held on by its own shape at every click and while it turns."
  option "Push-to-click dial on a keyed stub" "Push the tail: it clicks at 0, 15 and 30°, and the dial cannot come off."
  option "Click dial with a pull-tab latch" "v7: firm, but the dial dropped off its stub while the latch was pulled."
  option "Fixed angle" "Nothing to move or wear: reprint it to change the tilt."
}

decision oring_size "O-ring size" {
  chose: "AS568-227 (53.57 × 3.53 mm)"
  by: "claude"
  why: "Going round the double hooks takes a longer loop than two pins did. The thick 3.53 mm cord pulls about twice as hard as a 2.62 mm one at the same stretch, so one O-ring does the work of two; this size stretches about 35 % on the post, which fitting by hand can still manage."
  option "AS568-226 (50.39 × 3.53 mm)" { oring_id: 50.39mm, oring_cs: 3.53mm } "Tighter: more grip, a hard pull to fit."
  option "AS568-227 (53.57 × 3.53 mm)" { oring_id: 53.57mm, oring_cs: 3.53mm } "Balanced."
  option "AS568-228 (56.74 × 3.53 mm)" { oring_id: 56.74mm, oring_cs: 3.53mm } "Looser: easier on, less grip once it relaxes."
  option "AS568-130 (50.47 × 2.62 mm)" { oring_id: 50.47mm, oring_cs: 2.62mm } "Thin cord: easy on, but about half the grip."
}

// ── Checks ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// where the dial and the number bear on the jaw: the cheeks' inner faces round the dial and the stub it turns on (the pin
// sits clear of its holes, so the loads go into the jaw here, not into it)
let grip_w   = ((far_in + x_near) / 2, H_w.y, H_w.z)
let grip_sz  = (x_near - far_in + 0.4, 14, 13)

analysis "Crosswind gust" {
  parts: [bracket]
  // the saddle is only pressed on by the O-ring, through the pad: it must stay pressed and not turn on the post
  fix: contact(zone(at: post_mid, d: 2 * seat_r, axis: post_ax, length: saddle_h - 3), mu: pad_mu, grip: [oring])
  load: pull(oring)
  // the wind on the number comes into the jaw as a force and its moment
  load: wind(zone(at: grip_w, size: grip_sz), gust, dir: X, area: plate_l * plate_h - taper_l * (plate_h - tip_h) / 2, at: J_w + num_dir * (plate_l / 2))
  carries: mass(zone(at: grip_w, size: grip_sz), 11g)
  gravity
  printed
  safety: 2
}

// a knock bends the thin number over before it can pass much force on: at most its bending strength where it leaves the
// dial, about r_hub behind its centre hole
let number_bend = 30 * (plate_h * plate_t * plate_t / 6)          // N·mm, 30 MPa polypropylene
let knock_felt  = min(knock, number_bend / (plate_l - hole_c - r_hub))

analysis "Leg knock" {
  parts: [bracket]
  fix: contact(zone(at: post_mid, d: 2 * seat_r, axis: post_ax, length: saddle_h - 3), mu: pad_mu, grip: [oring])
  load: pull(oring)
  load: force(zone(at: grip_w, size: grip_sz), knock_felt, dir: X, at: J_w + num_dir * plate_l)
  printed
  cycles: 1000
  safety: 2
}

analysis "Pothole" {
  parts: [bracket]
  fix: contact(zone(at: post_mid, d: 2 * seat_r, axis: post_ax, length: saddle_h - 3), mu: pad_mu, grip: [oring])
  load: pull(oring)
  carries: mass(zone(at: grip_w, size: grip_sz), 11g)
  accel: (0, 0, -bump_g)
  printed
  safety: 2
}

// a hand pushing the number's tail past an end click: the dial's groove pushes the finger sideways with all the end click
// passes on (any more and it slips), and its bore pushes the stub the other way, a couple as big as the push's moment about
// the pin. The finger's point is finer than the analysis's elements, so its push goes in along its stem, from the blade
// through the window in the far cheek, whose side the stem bears on once it has moved 0.3 mm; the bore's goes on the stub
// inside the dial. Each zone starts at its at: and runs on along X, short of the faces beside it. The finger's own bending,
// stem and point, is checked on its own below
let tilt_turn = end_n * tail_arm / r_c
let f_z      = H_z - r_c
let finger_w = (blade_in, pivot_y * ct + f_z * st, -pivot_y * st + f_z * ct)
let stub_w   = (x_bed + 0.7, H_w.y, H_w.z)

analysis "Tilt push" {
  parts: [bracket]
  fix: contact(zone(at: post_mid, d: 2 * seat_r, axis: post_ax, length: saddle_h - 3), mu: pad_mu, grip: [oring])
  load: pull(oring)
  load: force(zone(at: finger_w, d: f_w + 0.2, axis: X, length: far_in - blade_in), tilt_turn, dir: (0, -ct, st))
  load: force(zone(at: stub_w, d: 2 * st_r + 0.4, axis: X, length: x_stub - x_bed - 1), tilt_turn, dir: post_back)
  printed
  cycles: 1000
  safety: 2
}

// the finger, bent at the click spring by the end click's sideways push; and its point, which takes all of that push at
// its very tip just as the click slips, and is bent hardest pt_gap back from the tip
let finger_mpa = 6 * tilt_turn * ((f_base + f_tip) / 2 - blade_in) / ((saddle_h / 2 + H_z - r_in) * f_w * f_w)
let point_mpa = 3 * tilt_turn * tan(flank)^2 / (8 * (saddle_h / 2 + H_z - r_in) * pt_gap)

require "The pin and its teardrop point pass through the centre hole" for knock: pin_d * 0.7071 + 0.15 <= hole_cd / 2
require "The pin goes on into the stub on the far cheek, so the number cannot slip past its tip" for knock: engage >= 0.6
require "The near cheek keeps at least 2 mm round the pin's hole" for knock: H_z + ha + near_e / 2 + 2 <= saddle_h / 2 && cheek_back - H.y - hd / 2 - near_e >= 2
require "The stub keeps at least 1.2 mm round the pin's hole" for knock: st_w - hd / 2 - far_e >= 1.2 && (st_r - far_e * tan(50)) * cos(50) - hd / 2 >= 1.2
require "The dial's lip stands at least 2 mm in front of the number's point" for knock: r_lip - hole_c - 0.2 >= 2
require "At its top click, the number's top front corner stays clear of the seatpost" for fit: corner_y >= 2
require "At its top click, the number's top front edge leaves the jaw inside the open cavity, so it slides in without catching" for fit: top_open >= cav_front
require "The tilt is one of the dial's clicks" for adjust: abs(rise - 15 * round(rise / 15)) < 0.01 && rise <= top_click
require "The dial only slides off its stub well past the top click, where the number cannot go: its top front corner would be inside the seatpost" for fit: fit_turn - key_slip >= 20 && pivot_y + (taper_l - hole_c) * cos(ja_fit - key_slip) - plate_h / 2 * sin(ja_fit - key_slip) < 0
require "A knock on the number pushes the dial back less than it takes to slip a click" for knock: x_bed - far_in < ride
require "The dial holds its tilt over a pothole, twice over" for bumps, adjust: hold_t >= 2 * pothole_t
require "Pushing the number's tail clicks it to the next tilt: firm, but light" soft for adjust: click_n >= 2 && click_n <= 5
require "Clicking strains the spring within what ASA takes for thousands of clicks" for adjust: 1.5 * spring_t * (squeeze + ride + tr_d) / spring_a^2 <= 0.7 * 0.025
require "The finger takes the end click's sideways push twice over, at its stem and at its point" for adjust: 34 / max(finger_mpa, point_mpa) >= 2
require "Pulling the spring's tab out to fit the dial is a firm pull, not a hard one" soft for fit: fit_n <= 25
require "The O-ring pulls firmly" soft for gust, bumps: oring.stretch >= 1.3
require "Stretching the O-ring over its hook is a one-hand job" soft for fit: oring.fit_force <= 45N
require "The hooks' rim keeps the O-ring on" for fit: lip >= 0.75 * oring_cs
require "The O-ring runs clear of the saddle's sides" for fit: clear >= oring.cord / 2 + 0.5
require "The saddle hugs the post's back corners" for gust: horn >= 2 && horn <= post_d / 3

test "Fit and remove": "Pull the click spring's tab out (beside the far cheek), hold the dial with its keyhole up and push it up into the jaw from below onto its stub, turn it tail-down and let go of the tab: it clicks into its top click and must not come off at any click. Hook one end of the O-ring over the left double hook (over the top prong, down behind, under the bottom one), wrap it round the post high and low and stretch the other end over the right hook's rim with one hand; press the arm's lever toward the post, slide the number into the dial's lip and let go: the pin must go through its centre hole into the stub. Press the lever again and slide it out."
test "Tilt": "Hand the bracket to someone who has not seen it and ask them to tilt the number: pushing its tail up or down, it must click at 0, 15 and 30°, stay put when the tail is tapped, and spring back into the end click when pushed a little past it."
test "Shake test": "Ride 10 minutes of rough road and a 50 km/h descent; the bracket must not turn on the post and the number must neither move in the jaw nor change its tilt."
buy "AS568-227 EPDM O-rings (spares)" { qty: 4, price: 0.6 }
